{"title": "CSCI-630: Foundations of Artificial Intelligence", "learningObjectives": [
        "Define the core components of the NIST AI Risk Management Framework and explain how each phase addresses specific AI risks.",
        "Explain the distinction between supervised and unsupervised machine learning workflows, emphasizing data leakage prevention and cross-validation.",
        "Apply a practical analysis checklist to evaluate AI system robustness, fairness, and transparency in real-world deployment contexts."
      ], "beats": [
        {
          "title": "Introduction to AI Foundations",
          "claim": "CSCI-630 introduces the theories and algorithms used to create artificial intelligence systems, covering search, logic, planning, machine learning, and applications in computer, ",
          "example": "Students will explore how search algorithms navigate decision trees to find optimal solutions in robotics navigation tasks.",
          "visualDirection": "Display a flowchart showing the course structure: search algorithms, logic, planning, machine learning, and application domains like computer vision and NLP.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-630"]
        },
        {
          "title": "NIST AI Risk Management Framework Overview",
          "claim": "The NIST AI Risk Management Framework organizes work around four phases: Govern, Map, Measure, and Manage.",
          "example": "In a healthcare AI deployment, the Map phase documents the intended use of a diagnostic tool and identifies affected patient populations.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a four-step circular diagram labeled Govern, Map, Measure, and Manage, with each node connected to a brief risk-management action.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ai-rmf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Govern Phase: Policies and Accountability",
          "claim": "The Govern phase establishes policies, responsibilities, and accountability for AI risk.",
          "example": "A university AI ethics board assigns specific roles for reviewing algorithmic bias in automated grading systems.",
          "visualDirection": "Create a table mapping governance roles (e.g., AI ethics board, compliance officer, developers) to accountability responsibilities.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ai-rmf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Map Phase: Context and Impacts",
          "claim": "The Map phase documents context, intended use, affected people, and potential impacts.",
          "example": "A self-driving car manufacturer maps the operational design domain, specifying weather conditions and traffic scenarios where the system will operate.",
          "visualDirection": "Illustrate a contextual mapping matrix with columns for context, intended use, affected people, and potential impacts.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ai-rmf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Measure Phase: Risk Analysis Methods",
          "claim": "The Measure phase uses qualitative and quantitative methods to analyze identified risks.",
          "example": "Researchers conduct A/B testing and statistical analysis to measure the fairness and accuracy of a facial recognition model across different demographics.",
          "visualDirection": "Display a split-screen graphic: left side shows qualitative risk assessment (e.g., stakeholder interviews), right side shows quantitative metrics (e.g., accuracy, bias scores).",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ai-rmf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Manage Phase: Prioritization and Monitoring",
          "claim": "The Manage phase prioritizes risks and allocates resources for response and monitoring.",
          "example": "A financial institution prioritizes high-risk algorithmic trading models for continuous monitoring and allocates resources for real-time anomaly detection.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a risk prioritization matrix with axes for likelihood and impact, highlighting high-priority risks requiring immediate monitoring.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ai-rmf"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Machine Learning Workflows: Supervised vs. Unsupervised",
          "claim": "Supervised methods learn a mapping from examples paired with target labels, while unsupervised methods identify structure without target labels.",
          "example": "A spam filter uses supervised learning with labeled email data, whereas customer segmentation uses unsupervised clustering to group users by behavior.",
          "visualDirection": "Present a side-by-side comparison chart: supervised learning shows labeled examples mapping to outputs, unsupervised learning shows raw data clustering into groups.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Preprocessing and Leakage Prevention",
          "claim": "Preprocessing must be fitted only on permitted training data to avoid leakage, ensuring consistent transformations via pipelines.",
          "example": "A medical imaging pipeline standardizes image dimensions and normalizes pixel values using only the training set statistics before validation.",
          "visualDirection": "Draw a pipeline diagram showing data flowing through preprocessing steps fitted on training data, then applied to validation and test sets without re-fitting.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Cross-Validation and Evaluation Metrics",
          "claim": "Cross-validation repeats training and validation across defined data splits, and evaluation metrics must match the task and the cost of different errors.",
          "example": "A fraud detection system uses k-fold cross-validation to assess model stability, with precision and recall metrics weighted to penalize false negatives heavily.",
          "visualDirection": "Illustrate a k-fold cross-validation cycle with data splits rotating through training and validation sets, alongside a metrics comparison table.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Practical Analysis Checklist for AI Deployment",
          "claim": "AI systems require ongoing monitoring because context and system behavior can change, and robustness, privacy, security, transparency, and fairness must be continuously evaluated.",
          "example": "A deployment checklist verifies that the AI system maintains fairness thresholds, logs privacy compliance, and updates monitoring alerts when distribution shift is detected.",
          "visualDirection": "Provide a printable checklist template with checkboxes for robustness, privacy, security, transparency, fairness, and monitoring status, aligned with NIST and ML workflow standards.",
          "sourceIds": ["nist-ai-rmf", "scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        }
      ]}
